{{ElectionsNY}}
The '''2002 New York gubernatorial election''' was held on November 5, 2002 to elect the governor and lieutenant governor of New York. [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] incumbent [[George Pataki]] was re-elected to a third term with 49.4 percent of the vote over state comptroller [[H. Carl McCall|Carl McCall]] and businessman [[Tom Golisano]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2002/ALLPOLITICS/11/05/elec02.ny.g.hotrace/|title=Pataki wins third term in New York - Nov. 5, 2002|website=edition.cnn.com}}</ref> McCall carried [[New York City]] but performed poorly in [[upstate New York]]. Golisano received 14.3 percent of the vote, carrying his home county of [[Monroe County, New York|Monroe]] and finishing ahead of McCall in 23 counties. 

As of {{CURRENTYEAR}}, this was the last time a Republican won any statewide election in New York, and this is the last time a Republican carried [[Westchester County, New York|Westchester]], [[Albany County, New York|Albany]], [[Onondaga County, New York|Onondaga]], and [[Tompkins County, New York|Tompkins]] counties in a gubernatorial election. This is also the longest drought nationally for the Republican Party to win in a statewide office. This was the first time since [[1978 New York gubernatorial election|1978]] that the winner of the gubernatorial election was of the same party as the incumbent president.

== Republican primary ==

===Candidates===
*[[George Pataki]], Governor of New York since 1995

====Declined====
*[[Rudy Giuliani]], former mayor of New York City (1994–2001)

=== Results ===
Pataki won the Republican nomination unopposed.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://www.btpm.org/2002-09-11/golisano-beats-pataki-in-independence-primary|title=Golisano Beats Pataki in Independence Primary|date=September 11, 2002|website=Buffalo Toronto Public Media}}</ref>

== Democratic primary ==
=== Candidates ===
* [[H. Carl McCall]], New York state comptroller

==== Withdrew ====
* [[Andrew Cuomo]], former [[United States Secretary of Housing and Urban Development|U.S. secretary of housing and urban development]] and son of former governor [[Mario Cuomo]]

=== Campaign ===
Comptroller [[Carl McCall]] defeated [[Andrew Cuomo]] at the Democratic state convention,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.recordonline.com/story/news/2002/05/24/mccall-gets-democratic-party-s/51171260007/|title=McCall gets Democratic Party's nod|website=Times Herald-Record|date=May 24, 2002}}</ref> and Cuomo withdrew from the race less than a week before the Democratic primary.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2002/09/04/nyregion/2002-campaign-announcement-cuomo-quits-race-backs-mccall-for-governorship.html|title=THE 2002 CAMPAIGN: THE ANNOUNCEMENT; CUOMO QUITS RACE AND BACKS MCCALL FOR GOVERNORSHIP|first=Shaila |last=Dewan|date=September 4, 2002|via=NYTimes.com}}</ref>

